From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:47:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: b43: HT-PHY: back off the TX power for the weakest rate

On BCM4331 boards without an external PA flag, the HT-PHY TX power
control loop holds every core at the SPROM maximum power, 19 dBm at
2.4 GHz on an Apple AirPort Extreme card. The SPROM asks for 1.5 dB
less at MCS 5-7 and 1 dB less at 48 and 54 Mbit/s, but the loop has a
single target, and b43 doesn't know how HT-PHY sends a single rate
below it: unlike N-PHY, writing offsets to entries 64-147 of table 0x1a
changes nothing. So 64-QAM goes out at the power meant for CCK and is
too distorted to decode: a sniffer and the AP saw 94-100% of the MCS 6
and MCS 7 frames with a bad FCS at -21 dBm.

Lower the loop target by the largest per-rate backoff the SPROM asks
for in the band. The other rates lose the same power, a fraction of a
dB to 2.5 dB depending on the band.

At 2.4 GHz that only takes the AirPort card from 19 to 17.5 dBm per
core, still too close to where its PA saturates for 64-QAM. Measured
with a fixed TX gain index per core (power control loop off), the
estimated output rises by about 0.5 dB per index step and flattens at
20.75 dBm on all three cores from index 12 down. A sniffer counting
frames with a bad FCS saw MCS 6 and MCS 7 come out clean only between
indices 28 and 24, about 13 to 15 dBm per core, and 58% of the MCS 7
frames corrupted at about 17 dBm and 97% of the MCS 6 frames at about
19 dBm. The power control loop itself holds its target within about
1.5 dB on every core, so the target is the problem, not the TSSI
estimate. So also cap the 2.4 GHz target at 15.5 dBm per core, about
5 dB below the PA saturation. Boards with a static TX gain (external
FEM or high-gain PA) don't use the target and are not affected.

On the AirPort card at 2.4 GHz, channel 6, the frames with a bad FCS go
from 100% to 6-8% at MCS 6 and from 94-98% to 14-17% at MCS 7. At 5 GHz,
channel 36 (2.5 dB backoff), TCP upload goes from 31-35 to 40-43 Mbit/s
and download stays at 35-45 Mbit/s; WPA2, SAE and GCMP-256 connections
still pass.

Fixes: 5949e0406041 ("b43: HT-PHY: setup TX power control")
Signed-off-by: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c | 59 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 58 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c
@@ -16,6 +16,10 @@
 #include "tables_phy_ht.h"
 #include "radio_2059.h"
 #include "main.h"
+#include "ppr.h"
+
+/* Highest TX power control target per core at 2.4 GHz, 15.5 dBm in Q5.2 */
+#define B43_PHY_HT_2G_MAX_TARGET	62
 
 /*
  * A front end module like the R6400's. BFL_EXTLNA and extpagain don't tell
@@ -661,13 +665,44 @@ static void b43_phy_ht_tssi_setup(struct
 	}
 }
 
+/* The largest per-rate TX power backoff the SPROM asks for at @freq (Q5.2) */
+static u8 b43_phy_ht_rate_backoff(struct b43_wldev *dev, u16 freq)
+{
+	struct b43_ppr ppr;
+	enum b43_band band;
+	u8 max, min = U8_MAX;
+	int i;
+
+	if (b43_current_band(dev->wl) == NL80211_BAND_2GHZ)
+		band = B43_BAND_2G;
+	else if (freq < 5100)
+		band = B43_BAND_5G_LO;
+	else if (freq < 5500)
+		band = B43_BAND_5G_MI;
+	else
+		band = B43_BAND_5G_HI;
+
+	b43_ppr_clear(dev, &ppr);
+	if (!b43_ppr_load_max_from_sprom(dev, &ppr, band))
+		return 0;
+
+	max = b43_ppr_get_max(dev, &ppr);
+	for (i = 0; i < B43_PPR_RATES_NUM; i++) {
+		/* Rates the band doesn't have, CCK at 5 GHz, stay 0 */
+		if (ppr.__all_rates[i])
+			min = min(min, ppr.__all_rates[i]);
+	}
+
+	return min < max ? max - min : 0;
+}
+
 static void b43_phy_ht_tx_power_ctl_setup(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 {
 	struct b43_phy_ht *phy_ht = dev->phy.ht;
 	struct ssb_sprom *sprom = dev->dev->bus_sprom;
 
 	u8 *idle = phy_ht->idle_tssi;
-	u8 target[3];
+	u8 target[3], backoff;
 	s16 a1[3], b0[3], b1[3];
 
 	u16 freq = dev->phy.chandef->chan->center_freq;
@@ -709,6 +744,28 @@ static void b43_phy_ht_tx_power_ctl_setu
 		b1[0] = b1[1] = b1[2] = -1393;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The power control loop holds one target for all rates, and how
+	 * HT-PHY sends a single rate below it is not known. Lower the target
+	 * by the largest per-rate backoff the SPROM asks for, which keeps
+	 * 64-QAM inside what the PA handles, at the cost of the same backoff
+	 * for the other rates.
+	 */
+	backoff = b43_phy_ht_rate_backoff(dev, freq);
+	for (c = 0; c < 3; c++)
+		target[c] -= min(target[c], backoff);
+
+	/*
+	 * That is not enough at 2.4 GHz: there the PA saturates at about
+	 * 20.75 dBm per core, and 64-QAM only stays decodable at least 5 dB
+	 * below that. With a static TX gain the target is not used.
+	 */
+	if (b43_current_band(dev->wl) == NL80211_BAND_2GHZ) {
+		for (c = 0; c < 3; c++)
+			target[c] = min_t(u8, target[c],
+					  B43_PHY_HT_2G_MAX_TARGET);
+	}
+
 	/* Regulatory and user limits, but no lower than 8 dBm */
 	limit = INT_TO_Q52(dev->phy.chandef->chan->max_power);
 	if (dev->phy.desired_txpower)
